In article <1992Jan22.224516.18331@samba.oit.unc.edu> Mike.Marcelais@bbs.oit.unc.edu (Mike Marcelais) writes:

>Set up the game in an account with no real special privledges and do a
>'setuid' (chmod 7555 nethack) When anybody runs nethack, they will run
>as if they were logged in as the owner of the file 'nethack' All the
>bones files and save games will be owned by this person.

well, not really. first, you mean "chmod 4555 nethack" - 7555 is
setuid, setgid, and sticky bit.

Second, although its not standard, it's really much better to make
nethack be setgid, and have the appropriate directories group writeable.  
That's "chmod 2775" or "chmod g+s".

Our system has a group games, and things like score files
and save directories are writeable by group games. Make nethack setgid
games, and then anyone who runs nethack can write those files, within
the context of the nethack executable. I never have understood why
more people don't install things this way.

It's not perfectly secure, but the worst that can happen is that users
can screw around with things writeable by group games, which is
usually not a problem.

>The reason not to give the file to an important account (like root) is
>because if the program isn't perfectly secure it will allow users
>access to that account.

Doesn't nethack have an exec a shell option? Did someone (dev team)
think to check that it ran the shell as the real user, not the
effective? Sure, I'd love to find a nethack setuid root.
